Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 2, verse 84:

When murder is the greatest of the major sins after shirk (polytheism), he follows it with a reminder of the covenant they made concerning it, and he joins to it the expulsion from homes, because wealth is the equal of life, and the dwelling is the greatest of wealth, and it is to the body what the body is to the soul. So he says: ﴿And when We took your covenant﴾ (p-9) "O Children of Israel: ﴿You shall not shed your blood﴾," meaning: none of you shall shed the blood of another;

﴿nor expel yourselves﴾ by one of you expelling another, because those connected by lineage or religion are like a single soul;

﴿from your homes.﴾ Al-Harrali said: its origin is what the Arabs enclosed of houses in a circle, to preserve what they contain of their property. End of quote.

And since they had recoiled at the demands of the command, and did not accept the good that had come to them, until they feared destruction by the collapse of the mountain (al-Tur) upon them, he alluded to that with His saying: ﴿Then you affirmed﴾, meaning: to all of that after reluctance and hesitation. Iqrar (affirmation) is the manifestation of commitment to what was hidden in its matter, as he said, al-Harrali.

﴿and you were witnesses﴾ to its obligation, and you were witnessing those great signs that compelled you to that. What has passed, which confirms this (p-10) from the Torah just now, contains sufficiency for the one who is granted success; and its remainder will come in al-Ma'ida, God willing, Exalted is He.
